questions.explanation_english:
Reciprocal Teaching is an instructional framework formulated by Annemarie Sullivan Palincsar and Ann L. Brown (1984) rooted in Vygotskian social constructivism and cognitive apprenticeship. Designed specifically to enhance reading comprehension and metacognitive monitoring, it structures dialogue between teacher and students using four specific cognitive strategies:
1. Predicting: Guessing what the author will discuss next using text clues and prior knowledge;
2. Questioning: Generating inferential and substantive questions about the text;
3. Clarifying: Identifying difficult vocabulary, ambiguous concepts, or structural complexities and resolving them;
4. Summarizing: Identifying and articulating the main ideas and core synthesis of the passage.
Initially, the teacher models these four strategies (scaffolding). Gradually, students take turns leading the discussion, fostering autonomous reading comprehension.
